          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Where do authorities differ on how lipid-treatment goals are expressed?",
          "answer": "The main difference is in how the goal is written down.\n\n**US framing:** According to the lipid screening and statin initiation guidance (a major cardiology society consensus summary), US guidance describes the target in terms of statin intensity and the percentage reduction in cholesterol from the starting level. It does not give a single numeric LDL target.\n\n**European framing:** The same document says European guidance gives explicit LDL goals. These goals get lower as the person's risk category rises. Where a statin alone does not reach the goal, combination therapy is used.\n\nThe guidance notes that \"at goal\" means different things under each framing, so the two should not be mixed in one answer. The retrieved text does not explain which framing is better or how the two would apply to any particular person.\n\nThe search also returned guidance on thyroid, influenza, migraine and diabetes. Only the diabetes guidance touches on a similar issue: some bodies state a single HbA1c number, while others describe an individualized range without a headline figure. That is a separate topic, so I have not used it above.\n\nThis is general information, not advice for you. If you want to know which target applies to you, a clinician who knows your history is the right person to ask.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
